course: dictionary
lesson: 8904
title: One word, many tracks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «shunted» is in this sentence: «the puffing and snorting of engines and the banging of shunted trucks». The clues around it are engines, trucks, and puffing, so the scene is a railway station. When you see a word you do not know, look at the words near it.
explain: Shunted trucks are trucks that have been pushed from one track to another. The banging is the noise of heavy wagons knocking together as they are moved about. Test your meaning by swapping it in: «the banging of pushed trucks» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Shunt can also be a noun, meaning a small car crash. The same word can work in different ways, so check the sentence to see which meaning fits. The clues tell you which one it is.
fact: Shunt means to push or move something to one side, often roughly.
fact: On a railway, to shunt a train or carriage is to move it from one track to another.
fact: To shunt a person means to send them somewhere less important, so they are out of the way.
fact: In everyday talk, a shunt is a small car crash where one car bumps into the back of another (UK informal).
fact: «Shunted aside» means pushed out of the way, often unfairly.
exercise: «the banging of shunted trucks» What does shunted mean in this sentence? | Stopped by a red light | Left at the station forever | *Pushed from one track to another | Painted bright red
exercise: Dad says, «The lorry shunted the car at the lights.» What does shunted mean here? | Gently waved past | *Bumped into the back of | Painted over with a new colour | Carefully parked beside
exercise: Ella says, «Tom was shunted off to the back of the class.» What does this suggest? | Tom was asked to lead the class | Tom got the best seat at the front | Tom was sent home early | *Tom was sent away from the action, out of the way
test: In «the puffing and snorting of engines and the banging of shunted trucks», what place is the sentence about? | A school playground | A farm | *A railway station | A beach
test: In «a shunt at the lights», what does shunt mean? | A long walk | A tiny tube inside the body | *A small car crash | A train full of passengers
exam: Grandad says, «I was shunted aside at work.» What does shunted aside mean? | Lifted high into the air | Chased by a dog | Praised in front of everyone | *Pushed out of the way, often unfairly
